WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.240
Hi, I'm Apolonia
Gardner, and I'm

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.532
a junior in computer science,
biology and neuroscience.

00:00:04.532 --> 00:00:06.532
And today I'm going to
tell you about my project

00:00:06.532 --> 00:00:07.520
on the striatum.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.380
So there are three
things you need to know.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.755
The striatum is
a brain structure

00:00:10.755 --> 00:00:12.170
linked to Huntington's disease.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.500
It contains two types of cells,
striosomes and matrix cells.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.983
And is directly involved in
cost benefit decision making.

00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:19.400
And so the goals
of my project are

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.192
to characterize the
roles of these two cell

00:00:21.192 --> 00:00:23.180
types in decision
making and also

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.340
to run wild type and
Huntington's disease mice

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.412
through these behavioral
studies to figure out

00:00:27.412 --> 00:00:29.120
if there's an underlying
circuitry change

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.620
in Huntington's mice.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.470
And so I'll first talk about
my calcium imaging results.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.990
So calcium imaging,
in general, is a way

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:38.810
that we can see cells
lighting up in the brain

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.720
and correlate that
activity with behavior.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.570
And so here I'm showing you four
different types of T-maze tasks

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.123
that we run our mice in.

00:00:46.123 --> 00:00:47.540
And each of them
are characterized

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.340
by different levels of costs
and rewards on either side.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.850
The rewards being different
levels of chocolate milk.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.580
And costs being different
intensities of light.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.730
And below I'm showing you heat
map plots of neural activity

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.110
for striosomes
and matrix neurons

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.440
in the four task types, where
red represents high activity

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.360
and blue represents
low activity.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.640
And so for example,
striosomes in the benefit,

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.220
benefit tasks seem to
be relatively active

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.490
regardless of whether the mouse
goes towards the pure chocolate

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.380
milk side or the mixture.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.975
Whereas, in the cost,
cost, striosomes

00:01:16.975 --> 00:01:18.350
seem to be relatively
active when

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.320
the mouse goes towards the high
cost side, but not the low cost

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.000
side.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.560
Behaviorally we see
a striking change

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.960
between Huntington's
and wild type mice.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.370
So Huntington's
disease mice seem

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.030
to be relatively repetitive
and fixed in their behavior.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.250
So given their choice
on a previous trial,

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.840
they tend to make
that same choice

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.195
on like the next
trial, two trials

00:01:38.195 --> 00:01:40.070
after, three trials
after, four trials after.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.370
Versus, wild type mice, which
their choices are largely

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.360
unaffected by the history.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.870
And lastly, we use
electrophysiology

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.090
to kind of visualize
the spike wave forms

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.260
that we're recording over here.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.070
So here I'm showing you two
different classes of neurons

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:56.212
in the striatum.

00:01:56.212 --> 00:01:57.920
And they have different
spike wave forms.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.780
This one goes up and then down.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.340
This one goes down and then up.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.990
So we already know that
there are different types

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.115
of neurons in the striatum.

00:02:04.115 --> 00:02:06.170
And that these belong
to different groups.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.470
And then based on the
time between spikes,

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.030
this neuron has very
small ties between spikes.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.900
So it fires really
fast and doesn't stop.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.950
This one has more variable
time between spikes.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.330
So it doesn't fire as reliably.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.920
And so based on
these properties,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.220
you can classify neurons
into different types.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.830
And so for future
directions, we're

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.770
going to run more mice
through this pipeline

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.330
to kind of resolve some of
these behavioral neural activity

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.413
trends better to see if we can
elucidate Huntington's disease.

